  "subtitle": "[ASSET: left | SlideRule | width=20%] There was no contest this month about the VMA, which goes to the \"built-in slide rule calculater\" shown in Iron Man's armor in [LINK:1963:TOS_50_iron0|TOS #50|2027-03-24].&nbsp; <i>Classic</i>!&nbsp; (But note that the word \"calculator\" is actually misspelled.)\n\n[ASSET: right | MoreRugged | width=12%] But we can't ignore the laugh-inducing panel in this month's [LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01], page 8 shows Don Blake closing his office early (to respond to a summons from Iron Man), only to show Jane Foster's thoughts of, \"Oh, Don, if only you didn't <i>pamper</i> yourself so!&nbsp; If only you were more <i>rugged</i>!\"&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>  Good enough for an Honorable Mention!",
  "review_text": "Nine graded stories this month, spread over eight issues (two again from Strange Tales), with a final weighted average of [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=x.xx],\n\nXxxxxxxxxxxx which is about a point-and-a-quarter improvement over the previous month's <b>4.51</b>.\n\n\nmeaning that, for the most part, the stories this time around were <i>well</i> above just the reader level, and in fact, 4 of the 9 stories were actually at mid-grade or above.&nbsp; It's clear from the letters coming in that there is an overall sense of excitement growing in the readership over the direction of the books in general.\n\nThe improved characterization of the X-Men was a noticeable plus this month, and the Hulk/Sub-Mariner \"super-villain team-up\" was solid also, and Electro's introduction was another in the positive column.&nbsp; While there was nothing really wrong with the FF, it and the other solo tales of Thor, Giant-Man and Iron Man did not elevate up enough into the realm of memorable, and on the negative side, the <i>lame</i> Human Torch and Doctor Strange offerings really make me wonder why I'm reading them at all!\n\nThe three main backup features that are at least <i>marginally</i> related to Marvel's main continuity are:\n[HANG] * Tales of Asgard\n[HANG] * The Wonderful Wasp Tells a Tale\n[HANG] * Tales of the Watcher\n\nThey all differ in the way they're told, with <i>Asgard</i> being a straight-forward 3<sup>rd</sup> person tale, while the Wasp seemingly makes hers up just to entertain her orphan listeners.&nbsp; On the other hand, the Watcher uses his platform to turn to the reader at the end and essentially say, \"And the moral of the story is...\"\n\nA couple of the split books also have completely unrelated backup stories, but one can already see the handwriting on the wall:&nbsp; they are slowly morphing over to feature mainline characters from the Marvel Universe, even though a couple of them still are simply the \"astonishing\" or \"suspenseful\" tales readers would have gotten <i>anyway</i>, simply dressed up to look as if they have some connection to the main continuity.&nbsp; (And, by the way, I'm not saying this is a <i>bad</i> thing -- but it <i>is</i> happening, whether readers like it or not.)\n\nBut what is unmistakably on the bad side this month:\n[BR] Longbox Playback archivists have recently uncovered evidence that Baron Mordo's earliest appearances were actually orchestrated by [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Baron Bored‑o, Dread Lord of Narrative Drag.] (And I hereby christen him as such -- I recommend you read a couple of my past reviews on issues he appears in!)&nbsp; Scholars disagree on whether Bored‑o acted alone or simply amplified Mordo's natural tendency toward plot‑induced tedium.&nbsp; But it makes little difference, as the net effect of his presence almost <i>guarantees</i> the reader a one-way trip to the Land of Nod.\n\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** SUMMARY_BEGIN ***\n*** THIS MONTH ***9 graded stories over 8 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 6.83 grade character issue    weight 6.5   xmen      XMEN #3  1.00 6.5   asm       ASM #9   1.00 6.0   ff        FF #23   1.00 6.0   iron      TOS #50  0.50 5.5   aven      AVEN #3  1.25 5.0   thor      JIM #100 0.50 4.5   giant     TTA #52  0.75 1.8   torch     ST #117  0.50 1.8   strange   ST #117  0.335.30*** LAST MONTH ***8 graded stories over 6 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 4.58 grade character issue   weight 6.5   asm       ASM #8  0.75 6.5   ff        FF #22  1.00 4.5   iron      TOS #49 0.75 4.0   thor      JIM #99 0.50 4.0   giant     TTA #51 0.50 2.0   asm       ASM #8  0.25 2.0   strange   ST #116 0.33 1.5   torch     ST #116 0.504.51\n*** SUMMARY_END ***]",
  "letters_page": "[HANG0] * Introduction of Electro, Mandarin, the Blob and Black Knight (Nathan Garrett -- not that <i>other</i> Black Knight!)\n[HANG0] * Sub-Mariner/Hulk \"super villain team-up\" in Avengers\n[HANG0] * Improved characterization of the X-Men, perhaps in response to readers who were saying they were no different than the FF, particularly the Beast.\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** INTRODUCTION_BEGIN ***\nBlack Knight (Nathan Garrett) | villain | Milestone: (first)\nBlob | villain | Milestone: (first)\nElectro | villain | Milestone: (first), (origin)\nNotes | first | Dr. Bromwell (Nicholas)\nNotes | first | villain | Terrible Trio (Bull Brogin, Yogi Dakor, Harry Phillips)\nMandarin | villain | Milestone: (first)\n*** INTRODUCTION_END ***]\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_BEGIN ***]\n[HANG0] * The Sub-Mariner joins the fun, forcing the Avengers to revisit their favorite pastime:&nbsp; fighting the Hulk.\n[HANG0] * FIXME - write something\n[HANG0] * FIXME - write something\n[HANG0] * Professor X's open-door mutant policy backfires spectacularly when the Blob arrives, but the team itself takes an important step forward.\n[HANG0] * FIXME - write something\n[HANG0] * FIXME - write something\n[HANG0] * The Human Torch takes on the Eel's return engagement, while readers wonder who asked for one.\n[HANG0] * Dr. Strange vs. Baron Mordo -- a replica house, a cylinder trap, and nothing resembling a motive.\n[HANG0] * FIXME - write something\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_END ***]",
  "ownership": "The end of the 1963 calendar year promises the following:\n[HANG0] * the Tomorrow Man is back to plague Thor\n[HANG0] * the Porcupine returns to annoy Giant-Man and the Wasp\n[HANG0] * the Human Torch has to contend with the Wizard yet again\n[HANG0] * Doctor Strange meets a bunch of possessed people\n[HANG0] * A terrible infant is on the loose, and the FF have to reign him in somehow\n[HANG0] * Iron Man is menaced by a scarecrow\n[HANG0] * and who the heck is the Big Man and his Enforcers?&nbsp; Let's hope that Spider-Man doesn't have to learn the hard way!"
